    "body": "## Overview\nThis issue tracks the creation of comprehensive documentation for advanced Git commands, including practical examples and use cases for each command.\n\n## Commands to Cover\n\n### 1. git stash\n**Purpose**: Temporarily save changes that you're not ready to commit.\n\n**Basic Usage**:\n```bash\n# Stash your current changes\ngit stash\n\n# List all stashes\ngit stash list\n\n# Apply the most recent stash\ngit stash apply\n\n# Apply a specific stash\ngit stash apply stash@{n}\n\n# Pop (apply and remove) the most recent stash\ngit stash pop\n\n# Drop a stash\ngit stash drop stash@{n}\n```\n\n**Example Scenario**:\nYou're working on a feature branch and need to switch to the main branch to fix a bug. Instead of committing incomplete work:\n```bash\ngit stash\ngit checkout main\n# Fix the bug\ngit checkout feature-branch\ngit stash pop\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. git cherry-pick\n**Purpose**: Apply specific commits from one branch to another.\n\n**Basic Usage**:\n```bash\n# Cherry-pick a single commit\ngit cherry-pick <commit-hash>\n\n# Cherry-pick multiple commits\ngit cherry-pick <commit-hash-1> <commit-hash-2>\n\n# Cherry-pick a range of commits\ngit cherry-pick <start-commit>..<end-commit>\n```\n\n**Example Scenario**:\nYou have a critical bug fix on the `bugfix` branch that you want to apply to the `main` branch without merging the entire branch:\n```bash\ngit checkout main\ngit cherry-pick a1b2c3d  # the commit hash of the bug fix\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. git revert\n**Purpose**: Create a new commit that undoes changes from a previous commit.\n\n**Basic Usage**:\n```bash\n# Revert a single commit\ngit revert <commit-hash>\n\n# Revert multiple commits\ngit revert <commit-hash-1> <commit-hash-2>\n\n# Revert without automatically committing\ngit revert --no-commit <commit-hash>\n```\n\n**Example Scenario**:\nA feature was merged but needs to be undone without rewriting history (important for shared branches):\n```bash\ngit revert abc1234  # This creates a new commit that undoes the changes\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. git reset\n**Purpose**: Undo commits and move the HEAD pointer to a previous state.\n\n**Basic Usage**:\n```bash\n# Soft reset: undo commit but keep changes staged\ngit reset --soft <commit-hash>\n\n# Mixed reset (default): undo commit and unstage changes\ngit reset --mixed <commit-hash>\n\n# Hard reset: undo commit and discard changes\ngit reset --hard <commit-hash>\n\n# Reset a specific file\ngit reset <commit-hash> <file-path>\n```\n\n**Example Scenario - Soft Reset**:\nYou committed but want to modify the commit before pushing:\n```bash\ngit reset --soft HEAD~1  # Undo the last commit, keep changes staged\n# Make additional changes\ngit commit  # Re-commit with modifications\n```\n\n**Example Scenario - Hard Reset**:\nYou made several commits that you want to completely discard:\n```bash\ngit reset --hard origin/main  # Discard all local commits, match remote\n```\n\n## Key Differences\n- **git revert**: Safe for shared branches; creates a new commit to undo changes\n- **git reset**: Rewrites history; use only on local or feature branches\n- **git stash**: Temporary storage; doesn't affect history\n- **git cherry-pick**: Selective commit application; useful for backporting fixes\n\n## Deliverables\n- [ ] Comprehensive documentation with all examples\n- [ ] Clear explanation of when to use each command\n- [ ] Warning about commands that rewrite history\n- [ ] Best practices and common pitfalls\n- [ ] Interactive examples or demo links (if applicable)",
